WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Binary stars orbit their common center of mass. When

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.060
a star approaches us, its spectral lines shift toward

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:10.100
blue; when it recedes, they shift toward red. Watch

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:13.760
the orbital motion and line position change together. Orbital

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.200
motion provides mass evidence. It is different from measuring

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:20.280
a color or an apparent brightness. The example is

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:25.020
schematic: a real mass determination needs sufficient orbital information,

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:29.100
including the viewing geometry. Radius follows a different relation:

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:33.280
luminosity is proportional to radius squared times surface temperature

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.320
to the fourth power. At fixed temperature, doubling radius

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.260
gives four times the luminosity. Keep the Stefan Boltzmann

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.704
law separate from binary dynamics.
